{"format":"megamek-board","version":2,"size":{"width":64,"height":132},"name":"Road and Rail Junctions","description":"Road/rail geometry fixture. The four 8 x 8 panels are road only (upper left), rail only (upper right), road + centered rail (lower left), and road + beside rail (lower right). Each panel contains exit masks 0 through 63, left to right then top to bottom. Bits 1,2,4,8,16,32 mean N,NE,SE,S,SW,NW. Every active arm has a connected terminal hex. Mask 0 is an isolated segment. Rails keep the standard legal straight and gentle bend pieces; adjacent-only arms remain stranded stubs, and Y tracks keep their standard stem. Beside choices apply only where the existing shared corridor permits them; they never invent a tight rail turn. The strip at row 70 compares independent crossings, side streets, partial shared bends and elevated rail. The strip at row 83 repeats a three-hex road triangle with straight, bending, centered, beside and elevated rail. Row 97 contains the exact Seaport left/right topologies and rotated/reflected copies of the left triangle. Row 111 compares the wider road with parking, roadside trees, a road bridge, centered and beside rail bridges, and a cliff tunnel. The north/south bridge seams expose the full road and ballast width. Row 119 compares an automatic priority T, an explicitly signal-controlled T, an automatic overhead-signal X, a STOP-only X, a roadside-signal X, and an overhead-signal X with centered railway. Row 125 adds centered and beside railway tunnels. Inspect the annotated centre hex of each case. All roads and rails are ordinary editable board terrain.","options":{"exitRoadsToPavement":false},"tags":["Example"],"notes":[{"at":[3,3],"text":["Road only; exits 0 (000000)"]},{"at":[7,3],"text":["Road only; exits 1 (000001)"]},{"at":[11,3],"text":["Road only; exits 2 (000010)"]},{"at":[15,3],"text":["Road only; exits 3 (000011)"]},{"at":[19,3],"text":["Road only; exits 4 (000100)"]},{"at":[23,3],"text":["Road only; exits 5 (000